[Unit]
Description=Estate host checks — addresses, CPU caps, the host and pool inventory, latency, disk headroom, that the alert receiver delivers, that the mail server's outbound chain is wired, and that the mail domains' SPF/DKIM/DMARC resolve, and that every generated group compose still matches the member repos it is built from, and that only `authentik Admins` grants Authentik superuser rights, and that every sign-in gate's callback fits its proxy's header buffer, and that the DMARC aggregate reports show every sending stream aligning (the evidence behind tightening the policy to p=reject)
Documentation=https://github.com/innotelinc/innotel-platform-stack/blob/main/docs/container-placement.md

# Each check reads the estate hosts over ssh, so the LAN has to be up first. None of
# them asserts anything about a local service; they are the estate's own invariants.
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=/opt/innotel/estate-checks

# TEXTFILE_DIR is the directory node-exporter's textfile collector mounts (see
# extensions/monitoring/docker-compose.host.yml). Each check writes its own file there;
# the collector turns them into the metrics `prometheus/rules/estate-checks.yml` alerts
# on. The default is the Cerulean edge, which already runs the per-host metrics stack.
#
# It is deliberately not under /root. It used to be, because the Authentik dump beside it
# was, and a `/root` cleanup would have taken every series in the estate with it silently
# — the rules key on series that exist, so their absence is exactly the silence they were
# written for. The path is node-exporter's own conventional one, which is also what the
# compose file mounts when TEXTFILE_DIR is unset; `docs/root-paths.md` carries the ordered
# re-apply, because the dump's own writer lives in the Cerulean repo and has to name the
# same directory.
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/innotel/estate-checks.env
Environment=TEXTFILE_DIR=/var/lib/node_exporter/textfile

# `-` on each ExecStart so a failing check does not stop the ones after it: all of them
# must publish every run, or a check that is merely unhappy today would take the others'
# metrics stale and page about the wrong thing. The finding is the textfile, not the
# unit's exit status. Reaching the hosts with a key is the clean case; where the estate
# uses a password instead, put SSHPASS in the environment file above (and install
# sshpass on this host). The key lives on the edge and a host is readable only when it is
# in that host's `authorized_keys` and named in the edge's `/root/.ssh/config` — see
# `scripts/trust-estate-hosts.py`, which installs both and whose `--check` reports drift.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-container-addresses.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-container-address.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-container-limits.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-container-limits.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-estate-inventory.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-estate-inventory.prom

# And that a container which is *running* would still be running after a reboot
# (2026-11-17). `boot.autostart=false` is the quiet half of every move: it is right while
# a copy is being parked and wrong the moment the move finishes, and nothing is visibly
# broken in between — the instance answers every probe and disappears at the next boot.
# All nine of `i2`'s instances carried it on the day the host rejoined the estate, and a
# reboot would have taken the media stack, its Prometheus, the NAS and the rest with it.
# `check-retired-instances.py` asserts the other direction (instances that must stay
# down); this asserts that everything else comes back, with `onyx`'s USB-disk dependency
# carried as a named exemption that is published as a note rather than passed over.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-container-autostart.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-container-autostart.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-address-latency.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-address-latency.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-host-disk.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-host-disk.prom

# And that a container's PID 1 is actually reaping (2026-11-15). Two containers leaked
# zombies for weeks and nothing reported it: `openmaic-mineru` on i1 and `linkwarden` on
# i4 each ran a supervisor as PID 1 that never called `wait()`, so every lost child stayed
# in the table. Neither was broken enough to page — the containers answered and every
# check above passed — but the leak is monotonic, and a `pids` limit is what eventually
# fails. The fix was `init: true` in both compose files; this counts the symptom, because
# that fix is a file on two hosts and nothing else would notice it being reverted. The
# warning line sits above i2's one deliberate `epmd` zombie (a k3s pod with no `kubectl`
# to change it), so a normal host stays quiet. Reads `i3` too: it is an estate host again.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-zombie-processes.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-host-zombies.prom

# And that a mountpoint is still the thing it is named for. On 2026-11-14 the vault's USB
# drive dropped off the bus and came back as a different device node: `srv-vault.mount`
# went inactive, `/srv/vault` stayed as an empty directory, and the read-only view, the
# nginx `:8080` door and the NFS export all kept answering from nothing. `nofail` is the
# right fstab option and `mountpoint` cannot tell "absent" from "fine"; a sentinel file on
# the filesystem can. This reads the mount, the sentinel and the ro view in one ssh call.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-vault-mount.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-vault-mount.prom
# SMART on the disk that carries the vault: the mount check proves the path is the
# vault, this one asks whether the disk under it is still trustworthy. Same host, same
# label-addressed device (the letter changed once and would again).
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-vault-disk.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-vault-disk.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-alert-delivery.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-alert-delivery.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-mail-auth.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-mail-auth.prom
# The mail server is reached at its own address (`mail`, 192.168.1.15, in the container
# address table), so the check keeps working when the container moves host — and it has:
# it is on `i2` since 2026-11-02, having been on `i1`, then `i4`, then `i3` (retired
# 2026-11-02) before that. The table is the authority for which host that is, not this
# comment. STALWART_USER/
# STALWART_PASSWORD come from the EnvironmentFile above; the check has no default
# credential and must never carry one.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-mail-relay.py --base http://192.168.1.15:8080 --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-mail-relay.prom

# And that the mail server can still *sign a person in*, which is the half none of the
# checks above asks. On 2026-11-06 the sign-in completed and every request after it 500d:
# Stalwart's only directory speaks OIDC, it initialises that directory once, and a failed
# discovery fetch is cached for the process lifetime — so every bearer token answers 500
# while anonymous requests answer 200, and the page says "Authentication Failed" after a
# correct Authentik login. The check drives the webmail's sso/start, asks Authentik to
# accept the exact callback, and requires an impossible bearer token to be *refused with
# 401*; 500 names the cached-directory failure and its recovery. The timer
# `stalwart-watchdog.timer` on the mail container performs that recovery; this is what
# says whether it is working. See docs/mail-sso-directory-2026-11-06.md.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-mail-sso.py --webmail https://mail.innotel.us --base http://192.168.1.15:8080 --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-mail-sso.prom

# And that the document the mail server reads at start-up is still there, still current and
# still servable. Since 2026-11-09 Stalwart fetches it from a copy inside its own container
# rather than from the identity provider, which is what stopped a single unreachable
# provider from poisoning the directory for the life of the process. The copy refreshes
# every fifteen minutes and *keeps its last good bytes* when a refresh fails — so an
# ageing copy, a dead proxy, an uninstalled DNAT and a permission regression that panics
# the server are all silent here: the mail server starts, and nobody can sign in. This check
# reads the container through `incus exec` on the host that runs it, because the container
# has sshd but not the key the checks hold; the same route `check-alert-delivery.py` uses.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-stalwart-oidc-cache.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-stalwart-oidc-cache.prom

# And the pieces that copy depends on, by the runbook's own preflight (2026-11-14). The
# check above reads the copy; this asks the eleven facts step 1 of
# `docs/mail-discovery-canonical.md` asks a person — the three units, the leaf's SAN against
# the upstream the proxy will actually use, the anchor's expiry, the DNAT rules, whether the
# provider answers from inside the container, both users' read access, and the bearer probe.
# Every half-done subset of that apply fails identically (a start-up fetch cached as failed,
# every bearer 500, anonymous still 200), which is why the facts are published rather than
# left to the moment somebody is already halfway through.
#
# It carries no second copy of the rules: the repo's `stalwart-oidc-discovery-setup.sh` is
# streamed into the container and its `preflight` is run there, so the series says exactly
# what the runbook says. That is why this unit needs `scripts/` beside it and not only
# `check-*.py` — the check exits 2 with that reason if the script is missing.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-stalwart-oidc-preflight.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-stalwart-oidc-preflight.prom

# The one check that is about this repo rather than about a host. The files in `groups/`
# are generated *and tracked*, so a member repo that moves on leaves the file a rebuild
# starts from behind — and this repo's CI only compares them when *this* repo changes,
# while the inputs live in the member repos. Both sides are taken from the remote: a
# shallow clone of the stack repo for the checked-in files, and `fetch-group-members.py`
# — the CI job's own step — for the member repos, so nothing here depends on a checkout
# on this host and a version of the scripts deployed years ago still compares that
# year's `main` against that year's repos. ~15 s a run (17 shallow clones, all small).
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-group-compose-freshness.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-group-compose-freshness.prom
# It needs the provider's API, and without it exits 2 — a red series rather than a pass,
# which is how it was found on 2026-11-16 having never actually run here: the host unit
# carried the check but no AUTHENTIK_API_URL/AUTHENTIK_TOKEN, so the estate's strongest
# identity rule was silently unrunnable. Both come from the EnvironmentFile above, and
# the URL is the provider's *published* address (`http://192.168.1.71:9000`): this host
# cannot route to the container's bridge address, and the public name 504s under load.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-authentik-privilege.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-authentik-privilege.prom

# A gate's callback, not its front page: oauth2-proxy's session cookie is larger
# than nginx's stock 4k header buffer, so a gated host answers 502 on the
# callback after the person has already signed in (2026-10-25). This probes
# every gated name's callback and reads each proxy block's buffer.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-sso-callbacks.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-sso-callbacks.prom

# And the half the check above cannot see: whether the provider will send the browser
# back at all (2026-11-16). `redirect_uri` is the one part of a sign-in that is not
# discovered at runtime — the app sends a value, Authentik compares it byte for byte,
# and a hostname that moved without its registration being updated produces
# "Redirect URI Error" while the app answers 200 and the provider answers 200. Found by
# hand twice; three live Telno names were carrying sign-ins nobody could finish when it
# was finally run as an audit. Two rules on two directions: every registered URI must be
# served by an NPM host and accepted by the provider, and every hostname that starts an
# OIDC flow must be asking for a URI that is registered.
#
# It reads the provider at the container's own address: the public name answers 504
# through the edge under load, which would report every registration as unreadable. A
# transport error is a warning after one retry, not a failure — on 2026-11-16 two
# correct registrations failed the sweep with TLS resets and passed on the next probe,
# and patching either would have been a change to something that already worked.
# No `--docker` here on purpose: the check works out for itself whether the CLI is local
# or behind `incus exec proxy` (see its `docker_prefixes`). A quoted prefix in this file was
# split into six arguments by systemd, which is a silent way for an audit that nobody is
# watching to exit on argparse rather than run.
#
# `--auth` is the provider's *published* address on the edge container (9000 is mapped),
# not the container's own bridge address and not the public name: the bridge is not
# routable from this host at all, and the public name answers 504 through the edge under
# load, which reports every registration as unreadable. Measured 2026-11-16.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-sso-redirects.py --auth http://192.168.1.71:9000 --deadline 420 --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-sso-redirects.prom

# And whether a sign-in on the Orion NAS door can actually finish (2026-11-14). The proxy
# trusts an issuer, the estate serves one authentik under two names, and each name reports
# ITSELF — so the failure with no external signature is "the proxy trusts a different name
# than the one it sends people to". The login completes at the provider and dies at the
# callback with a generic 500, which is also what a bogus code produces, so probing the
# callback proves nothing. This reads the proxy's own env file on the edge, the issuer's
# discovery document, and the redirect the door emits, and names which of the three
# disagrees. `docs/identity-hosts.md` says why the brand question is a warning.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-orion-signin.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-orion-signin.prom

# The other half of the mail story: `check-mail-auth` proves the records are in the
# zone, this reads the reports receivers send back and says which sending streams do
# not align — the evidence behind tightening `_dmarc` to `p=reject`, which fails
# silently when it is wrong. DMARC_USER/DMARC_PASSWORD come from the EnvironmentFile
# above (the `rua` mailbox); the check has no default credential and must never carry
# one. Silence is read by age, not as a blanket "could not answer": within three days
# of the `rua` being published (--wired, default 2026-10-29) there is nothing to report
# on yet and it is a note, and after that it is a failure — the reporting path, not the
# policy, is what breaks that way. An unreadable mailbox is exit 2 either way.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-dmarc-alignment.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-dmarc-alignment.prom

# The edge's own two ways of losing a door without saying so (2026-10-29): a proxy
# host left pointing at a target that stopped existing (a loopback target from
# before the SSO gateway moved into its own network namespace — 502 on
# `admin.monarch.innotel.us` while the door beside it looked healthy), and a proxy
# host that drifted off the target `scripts/edge-vhost-targets.tsv` pins. Reads the
# generated blocks where the edge keeps them (the `proxy` container's volume) and
# TCP-connects every target; the discard port is reported as parked, not dead.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-edge-vhost-targets.py --config-dir /usr/src/proxy/data/nginx --expect /opt/innotel/estate-checks/edge-vhost-targets.tsv --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-edge-vhost-targets.prom

# And the other way a door is lost without a word (2026-10-30): NPM's own record of a
# host and the file it wrote are two copies of one fact, and neither write direction
# held. Twenty-five rows were deleted and their files stayed behind, still proxying into
# ports that no longer answered; parking `resend.innotel.us` set the row to port 9 while
# `220.conf` kept 8099. `check-edge-vhost-targets` saw the dead targets but not the
# cause — a door NPM cannot see. This compares the API's host list against every
# generated `.conf` and fails on an orphan file, a missing file, a target that differs
# between the two, and a disabled host whose file still serves. The row side is the API
# rather than `database.sqlite` because this NPM is MySQL-backed; NPM_ADMIN_EMAIL /
# NPM_ADMIN_PASSWORD come from the EnvironmentFile above (there is no default
# credential and it must never carry one), and a missing one is exit 2, not a pass.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-npm-config-drift.py --api http://192.168.1.71:81 --data-dir /usr/src/proxy/data --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-npm-config-drift.prom

# And that a moved workload stays moved (2026-11-16). `monarch` left `i1` because one
# spindle could not carry it, and the enforceable half of that move is the *name*: the
# instance was renamed `monarch-retired`, stopped, and its autostart disabled, so stale
# `incus start|exec|copy monarch` fails with "Instance not found" instead of reloading the
# spindle. Nothing would notice that being undone — a recreated instance answers perfectly,
# and the outage it brings is on a disk no other check reads. `incus-guard` cannot cover
# it: the lease only binds sessions that ask for it. This reads the hosts and asserts both
# halves: the retired instance present and stopped, and the live name absent.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-retired-instances.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-retired-instances.prom

# And that a pin holds in NPM's own record, not only in the file nginx reads
# (2026-11-09). `check-edge-vhost-targets` compares the declaration to the generated
# `.conf`; `check-npm-config-drift` compares the row to that same `.conf`; neither ever
# compared the declaration to the row. So the TV door was repaired in its file while the
# panel's row still named a Monarch SSO gateway, and two hours later the row regenerated
# the file: both checks then read two copies that agreed with each other and not with the
# estate. The row is the authority — NPM rewrites the file from it with no announcement —
# so a door whose row drifted is a door that is about to be rewritten. Reads only; the
# same script's `--apply` puts a drifted pin back through the API, which updates the row,
# the file and the running nginx in one write. NPM_ADMIN_EMAIL/NPM_ADMIN_PASSWORD come
# from the EnvironmentFile above.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-edge-vhost-pins.py --api http://192.168.1.71:81 --config-dir /usr/src/proxy/data/nginx --pins /opt/innotel/estate-checks/edge-vhost-targets.tsv --redirect-pins /opt/innotel/estate-checks/edge-redirect-targets.tsv --check-destinations --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-edge-vhost-pins.prom

# The media estate's doors, from the outside: `media.innotel.us` has to answer with
# Jellyfin's own page (published, not gated, since 2026-09-18) and `iptv.innotel.us` has to
# answer with Jellyfin's web client plus the Gizmo splash. The watch door is deliberately
# not a redirect — TV and native clients cannot complete a browser OIDC flow — so nothing
# else in the estate would notice it going dark, and a dead door there means a title plays
# nowhere. `tv.innotel.us` is Sonarr as of 2026-11-11, which is why the watch assertions
# follow `iptv` — Jellyfin's direct 14016 publish — rather than the name they used to.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-media-doors.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-media-doors.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-media-apps.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-media-apps.prom

# And that the door still opens for the people it is for: drives
# `POST /api/tokens/sso` inside the edge container as the gateway's hop, where a
# learner in the required group has to get a token and an identity outside it — or
# one forged at the published admin port — has to be refused. The 2026-10-29
# sign-in outage was a peer map reading `$remote_addr` after realip had rewritten
# it; every case here still said 403, and nothing was watching.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-edge-sso-door.py --docker 'incus exec proxy -- docker' --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-edge-sso-door.prom

# And the learner-facing door itself (2026-11-14): `class.atheniq.courses` is where every
# classroom link in `atheniq-work` goes, and it has to be *behind* a sign-in rather than
# merely up. A door that answers 200 anonymously reads as healthy everywhere else — the app
# is fine, the edge is fine, the page loads — so the only thing that reports it is a probe
# that insists on the redirect, on `client_id=classroom`, and on the callback naming the
# door it came from. It asks `/classroom/` as well as `/`, because a gate written on the
# landing page alone still hands a learner a 404 from the app. It holds the superseded
# `classroom.` name to one of its two allowed states (gated the same way, or moved to the
# classroom): a third state is a second application nobody registered, and the retirement
# order is `docs/atheniq-classroom-name.md`.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-atheniq-doors.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-atheniq-doors.prom

# And that no NFS share has been mounted twice or gone stale. On 2026-10-29
# `/srv/onyx-pool` in `distro` held 32 769 entries: ganesha restarted, every handle
# went stale, `mountpoint` blocks on a stale handle instead of failing, and the
# minute timer therefore mounted another copy on top each run until every `df` on the
# then-host (i3, retired 2026-11-02, so the mount stack reads `i1`, `i2` and `i4` now)
# queued behind the probes and the control plane's health probe could not be
# answered. It reads each host and then each running container *inside* the
# container — a container's mountpoints are not paths on its host — and bounds every
# probe at five seconds.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-mount-stacks.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-mount-stacks.prom

# And what the mail server actually *did* in the last day. Everything above asks
# whether a thing is configured; this reads Stalwart's own log for the traffic —
# delivered, bounced, queued, who authenticated, what was blocked — because no
# other check can answer "how much mail moved" and the Grafana mail dashboard has
# no other source (Stalwart v0.16 exposes no Prometheus endpoint). The container is
# found by asking each host what it runs, so the mail server moving host does not
# take this check with it. It publishes its counts as `innotel_estate_check_mail_*`
# beside the shared contract; the panels are dashboard `mail.json`.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-mail-traffic.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-mail-traffic.prom

# And the one process question none of the above asks: has anything been *stuck* for
# over an hour. On 2026-10-27 i4 sat at load 13 because a `docker exec` had been running
# `grep -rn TEXTFILE_DIR /` for 18 h 20 m and nginx carried a worker shutting down for
# 8 h — nothing misconfigured, so no estate check could see it. Written on the edge the
# same day, it ran there by hand and was never brought into the repo or this unit, which
# is why the dashboard's "Checks reporting" number was one behind the unit's own list.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-stuck-processes.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-stuck-processes.prom

# The doors onto the development guest that was 192.168.1.93, written after the Windows
# box at .17 dropped about half the connects it proxied while the VM itself looked
# healthy. The guest was retired on 2026-11-14 and `fax-staging.lab.innotel.us` went with
# it; `deploy.innotel.us` returned on 2026-11-15 pointing at the Coolify panel's own host
# (`192.168.1.113:8000`, no tunnel), which is what the check now probes and what the
# series reports. See check-deploy-door.py.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-deploy-door.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-deploy-door.prom

# And that the Telno node still holds the records its legacy database has (2026-11-02).
# The console and the Asterisk-era database are two copies of one fact with no live link:
# `scripts/import-legacy.mjs` copies devices, extensions, confirmed directory listings and
# brand strings once, and is idempotent, so on a healthy node it reports nothing but
# `exists`. A row deleted in the console, or a record that arrived in the legacy database
# afterwards, changes nothing about whether any service is healthy — the only symptom is a
# missing name on a phone — so nothing else in the estate could see it. The node runs the
# same script daily from `telno-legacy-import-check.timer`, but that verdict only reaches
# the node's own journal; this publishes it as a series beside every other check. It reads
# the node through `incus exec` because the container has no sshd, and it asks the running
# image's own importer rather than reimplementing what "present" means here.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-telno-legacy-import.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-telno-legacy-import.prom

# The one scheduled job that retries faster than anything here reports, and the reason it
# gets a series instead of a notification: `sync-mail-identities` runs every fifteen
# minutes (`docs/estate-alerts-ntfy.md` explains why it is not wired to `OnFailure=`), so a
# failure there is a standing condition measured in days, not an event. This reads the
# timer's own verdict inside the `mail` container — the same `incus exec` hop
# `check-alert-delivery.py` uses — so a mailbox with no identity to sign in is a page
# rather than a journal line the next run overwrites.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-mail-identities.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-mail-identities.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-installed-checks.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-installed-checks.prom
# The same comparison for every other unit this host carries: the ones in `systemd/`
# that are installed here, against the revision. A unit edited on a host during an
# incident leaves the job running and the repository wrong.
# i1 and i3 are read over ssh: only the edge's node_exporter is scraped, so a textfile
# written on either host would be read by nobody. `i3` joined the list on 2026-11-16 — it
# came back as an Incus host on 2026-11-15 and carries six of the containers that were on
# `i2`, so leaving it out meant a unit edited where `signara` and `magnate` run was
# compared nowhere. `i2` left the same day it was decommissioned (§19 of
# `docs/estate-rebalance-2026-11-15.md`): a host in this list that cannot answer costs every
# run a 25-second timeout and reports the decommission as drift.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-unit-drift.py --host i1=192.168.1.51 --host i2=192.168.1.52 --host i3=192.168.1.53 --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-unit-drift.prom

# And that a credential the estate depends on is not older than its rotation window
# (2026-11-17). Every other check asks whether a credential still *works*; none asked how
# old it is, and on 2026-11-16 a pair had to be replaced because it had been exposed — a
# replacement that turned out to be dashboard-only (`docs/mailjet-key-rotation.md`). A
# secret that is never rotated and never broken looks identical to one rotated yesterday,
# so the only witness is the vault's own `updated_time`. It reads KV v2 **metadata** and
# never the values, with a read-only token limited to `cerulean/metadata/*`; VAULT_ADDR and
# VAULT_TOKEN come from the EnvironmentFile above. A missing token is exit 2, not a pass.
# `--all` ages every top-level secret the mount lists rather than a hand-kept list, so a
# credential added tomorrow is covered without this line changing. Directories in the
# listing are skipped; their children are named with `--path` when one of them matters.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-credential-age.py --all --mount cerulean --max-age-days 60 --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-credential-age.prom

# And the shape of the history rather than the moment (2026-11-17). The alerts read the
# current value, which is right for paging and blind to a check that goes red and green
# every few runs: on 2026-11-16 `media_doors` and `sso_redirects` each produced a red run
# that repaired itself, and only a hand comparison found it. This reads Prometheus's range
# API for the series every check already publishes and warns when one changed status more
# than once in the window. Prometheus is the edge's own (`:9090`); an unreachable one is
# exit 2, because a flap report that cannot read history is not a clean bill of health.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-estate-flaps.py --days 7 --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-estate-flaps.prom
